$g(x)$ is the quotient of two, more basic, expressions: $\cos(x)$ and $\sin(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $g$ can be found using **the quotient rule**:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}\right]&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[u(x)]v(x)-u(x)\dfrac{d}{dx}[v(x)]}{[v(x)]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}
\end{align}$

Let's differentiate!

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}g'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{\cos(x)}{\sin(x)}\right]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[\cos(x)]\sin(x)-\cos(x)\dfrac{d}{dx}[\sin(x)]}{[\sin(x)]^2}&&\gray{\text{The quotient rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-\sin(x)\cdot\sin(x)-\cos(x)\cdot\cos(x)}{\sin^2(x)}&&\gray{\text{Differentiate }\cos(x)\text{ and }\sin(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-[\sin^2(x)+\cos^2(x)]}{\sin^2(x)}&&\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{\sin^2(x)}&&\gray{\text{Pythagorean identity}}
\end{align}$

Note:  Since $\dfrac{\cos(x)}{\sin(x)}=\cot(x)$, we have just shown that $\dfrac{d}{dx}[\cot(x)]=-\dfrac{1}{\sin^2(x)}$.
